+  "findings_addressed": [
+    "FIX-6 [P2] 1/1 — ORIGINLESS DIRECT COMPONENTS BYPASSED THE TIER ORACLE (scripts/sbom-survey/tests/test_cyclonedx.py, AC-SBOM-11). Authorized as a bounded round by Steward ruling seq-162: one finding, no sweep. The criterion's VALUE leg iterated the emitted components and did `if len(origins) != 1: continue`, which was written for the multi-origin case (a package declared by two manifests of different tiers has no single ruled answer) but SILENTLY SWALLOWED THE ZERO-ORIGIN CASE TOO. That is a hole with a specific exploit: a Slice-32 run_survey() could read an UNTRACKED / GITIGNORED manifest — python/pyproject.toml is the exact case REQ/AC-SBOM-02 exists to prevent — emit those dependencies WITH NO fathomdb:declared-in origin, and pass BOTH this tier oracle AND the discovery-boundary checks added at fix-4, because those checks can only constrain the origins that are actually PRESENT ('every origin must be a path git ls-files reports' says nothing about a component that carries no origin at all). The vacuity guard did not catch it either: one other component carrying an origin satisfies `assert graded`. So an entire untracked dependency tree could reach the BOM ungraded, untiered-in-effect, and invisible to the very criteria written to keep the survey inside `git ls-files`. CLOSED by splitting the zero-origin case out and ASSERTING it rather than skipping: a component tagged fathomdb:depth == 'direct' with NO origin FAILS, with a message that names the untracked-manifest-leakage class it guards, cites §5.5 and points at AC-SBOM-02. The grounding is definitional rather than invented: §5.5 already defines depth = 'direct' IFF the package name appears in a dependency table of at least one TRACKED, NON-EXCLUDED manifest, so a direct component with no declaring manifest CONTRADICTS THE SPEC — this fix does not add an obligation to Slice 32, it stops the suite from tolerating a violation of one §5.5 already stated.",
+    "FIX-6 — HOW THE LEGITIMATE ORIGINLESS CASE WAS KEPT PASSING, which is the whole care of this fix. Lockfile-only TRANSITIVES have no declaring manifest BY CONSTRUCTION (§5.5): they exist only because a lockfile-derived library<->library edge put them there, and the five pinned crates AC-SBOM-12 requires to arrive tagged `transitive` (proc-macro2, quote, syn, unicode-ident, serde_derive) are exactly that population. A blanket 'every component must carry an origin' would have made AC-SBOM-11 and AC-SBOM-12 MUTUALLY UNSATISFIABLE — AC-SBOM-12 demands those crates be present, AC-SBOM-11 would have forbidden them — so Slice 32 could not have turned the suite green at all. The assertion is therefore CONDITIONED on depth == 'direct' and reads `assert depth != \"direct\"`, so zero-origin `transitive` components remain exempt and the failure message says so explicitly ('Transitive components with no origin are legitimate (§5.5) and remain exempt'). The MULTI-ORIGIN skip is also untouched: `if len(origins) != 1: continue` still follows, so a package declared by two manifests of different tiers still has its VALUE ungraded, exactly as fix-4 ruled. The depth is read with a `next(..., None)` default rather than an index, so the new branch cannot itself raise on a malformed component — AC-SBOM-12 is the criterion that owns 'every component carries exactly one fathomdb:depth in {direct, transitive}', and duplicating that grade here would have been the sweep this round was forbidden.",
+    "FIX-6 COUNT — N DID NOT MOVE. An assertion inside AC-SBOM-11's own stated subject ('the tier that reaches the BOM must be the tier the rules assign'), not a new criterion: still 23 criteria, 23 tests, 23 failures, 0 passed, 0 skipped, 0 errors. No id minted, none retired; design §4 header and table, design §8 gate 6, scripts/sbom-survey/README.md, dev/DOC-INDEX.md and this JSON all still read 23. The last count move remains fix-3's 22 -> 23 (§12).",
+    "FIX-6 SCOPE — DELIBERATELY MINIMAL. Two files changed, 78 insertions / 12 deletions, readable in one screen. No sweep, no refactor, no unrelated strengthening, nothing under dev/plans/runs/codex/ (the six transcripts are now tracked and are the orchestrator's), no edit to plan-0.8.20.md, release-state, PROGRAM-SEQUENCING, STATUS or any ledger. Round 8 on this finding is FORBIDDEN by Steward ruling; this is the correct minimal fix and it is reported honestly rather than padded.",
+    "FIX-5 [P2] (round 5, STILL CLOSED) — OVER-SPECIFIC CLI ORACLE FOR THE UNTIERED-MANIFEST ERROR (scripts/sbom-survey/tests/test_cli.py, AC-SBOM-21). THIS FINDING IS THE INVERSE OF THE CLASS THE PREVIOUS FOUR ROUNDS FIXED. Every one of the ten earlier findings was a test too PERMISSIVE — it would let a wrong implementation pass. This one is too STRICT: it would REJECT A CORRECT IMPLEMENTATION. The criterion drives the CLI with a tier map whose single rule excludes dev/release/fixtures/ and therefore covers NO real manifest, and REQ-4 then requires only that the CLI NAME THE OFFENDING PATH on stderr. The assertion was `assert \"Cargo.toml\" in proc.stderr`, which accepts exactly one basename. A Slice-32 implementation that walks discovered paths in `git ls-files` order legitimately fails FIRST on Cargo.lock — or on any other tracked manifest, since the order in which the tool reaches an untiered path is an implementation choice REQ-4 does not constrain — names that path, satisfies REQ-4 in full, and STILL FAILS THIS TEST. CLOSED by asserting that stderr names SOME MEMBER OF THE SET of currently-untiered tracked manifests: the 21 tracked manifest paths that lie outside dev/release/fixtures/ (29 tracked manifests minus the 8 skew fixtures the one rule excludes), DERIVED FROM GIT through the suite's existing conftest.tracked_manifest_paths() helper rather than written as a second literal list — a hand-maintained list is exactly what drifted in rounds 1-3 (the missing setup.cfg, the stale 28). A VACUITY GUARD fails the criterion if that set is ever empty, since a tier map that happened to cover the whole repository would leave the assertion grading nothing. The failure message prints the full expected set alongside the actual stderr, so a Slice-32 implementer sees immediately what would have satisfied it.",
+    "FIX-5 (round 5, STILL CLOSED) — AC-SBOM-07 CHECKED FOR THE SAME DEFECT AND DOES NOT HAVE IT. The round-5 steer asked whether the sibling unit-level criterion (test_tiering.py::test_untiered_manifest_raises_untiered_manifest_error, which asserts UntieredManifestError names the path) carries the same over-specificity. It does not, and the reason is structural rather than incidental: TierMap.classify() is handed EXACTLY ONE path by the test ('some/brand/new/Cargo.toml'), so the error it raises can only be about THAT path — there is no discovery order, no set of candidates and no choice of victim, which means naming it is the SOLE correct behaviour and asserting it rejects nothing correct. The over-specificity is only possible where THE TOOL PICKS THE PATH ITSELF, which in this suite is the CLI alone. No change was made to AC-SBOM-07, and design §5.3 now states the distinction so it is not re-litigated.",
+    "FIX-5 COUNT — N DID NOT MOVE. This is an assertion correction inside an existing criterion, not a new one: still 23 criteria, 23 tests, 23 failures, 0 passed, 0 skipped, 0 errors. No id minted, none retired, and no statement of the number was touched — design §4 header and table, design §8 gate 6, scripts/sbom-survey/README.md, dev/DOC-INDEX.md and this JSON all still read 23. The last count move remains fix-3's 22 -> 23 (§12).",
+    "FIX-5 SCOPE — DELIBERATELY MINIMAL, AT THE ROUND CAP. Two files changed, 60 insertions / 8 deletions. No sweep, no refactor, no strengthening of unrelated assertions, no restructuring: the next review is round 5's successor at a hard budget that halts at round 6, and a broad diff now would open new surface and cost the slice its close. The only extension of scope taken was the one the steer permitted — checking AC-SBOM-07 for the same defect (it does not have it).",
+    "FIX-4 [P2] 1/2 (round 4, STILL CLOSED) — THE INJECTED PUBLISHED-SOURCE WAS NEVER EXERCISED THROUGH run_survey (test_registry.py, AC-SBOM-17). The criterion called classify_status() DIRECTLY, so Slice 32 could ship a correct comparator while run_survey(..., published=...) never called published.latest() and left every staleness row 'unknown' — and AC-SBOM-15/16 would still pass, because both inject a source that legitimately yields 'unknown' for everything. CLOSED by a second leg that drives a POSITIVE StaticSource THROUGH run_survey and asserts on the resulting staleness rows: 'current', 'outdated' and 'ahead' must all appear, each carrying the INJECTED latest_version, while every package absent from the mapping stays 'unknown' / None (which is what stops a blanket fill). Probes are taken from a preceding offline run rather than hardcoded, and constrained to a unique (ecosystem, name) and a plain major.minor.patch with major >= 1 so both sentinels (9999.0.0, 0.0.1) order unambiguously under BOTH comparators. Design §5.4 carries the probe table.",
+    "FIX-4 [P3] 2/2 (round 4, STILL CLOSED) — THE --describe TIER VOCABULARY WAS NOT ASSERTED (test_cli.py, AC-SBOM-19). §5.9 requires --describe to publish the vocabulary, but the assertions passed if the command omitted `tiers` entirely or returned wrong values. CLOSED by asserting `tiers` is PRESENT and that tuple(described['tiers']) == ('shipped', 'dev-tooling', 'eval-only') in that ruled order, with the order now stated in §5.9 so test and spec cannot drift.",
+    "FIX-4 SWEEP (round 4, STILL CLOSED) — all 23 criteria were swept for the class 'a criterion is graded against a helper while the integration boundary that must call it goes ungraded', which had appeared in three separate rounds. Five further instances found and closed: AC-SBOM-01 and -02 (git-derived scope asserted at the survey boundary via fathomdb:declared-in), AC-SBOM-11 (the BOM's fathomdb:tier must EQUAL TierMap.classify(<declaring manifest>).tier, not merely be a vocabulary member), AC-SBOM-20 (the sbom.cdx.json the CLI WROTE goes through the independent upstream validator) and AC-SBOM-22 (the written staleness.json envelope, which is what Slice 33 actually opens). The full 23-row table, including the rows that legitimately needed nothing and AC-SBOM-18 where a boundary leg was CONSIDERED AND DECLINED, is in design §13.2. Untouched by fix-5.",
+    "FIX-3 [P2] (round 3, STILL CLOSED) — NO ORACLE FOR LONGEST-PREFIX TIER MATCHING. §5.3 freezes matching as LONGEST-PREFIX-WINS AND ORDER-INDEPENDENT, but nothing graded it, so Slice 32 could implement FIRST-MATCH-WINS and pass the entire suite. CLOSED by AC-SBOM-23 on an overlapping pair chosen so the two strategies DISAGREE, with four assertions: longest wins; longest MATCHING not longest present; order-independence proved by building the same rule set in BOTH orders; and duplicate prefixes rejected at LOAD time with the prefix named.",
+    "FIX-2 [P2] 1/3 (round 2, STILL CLOSED) — CycloneDX validity was SELF-CERTIFIED. The oracle is the UPSTREAM cyclonedx-python-lib JsonStrictValidator(SchemaVersion.V1_6) with a known-invalid negative control (which, from fix-4, runs INSIDE the helper so every caller gets an oracle already shown to bite); sbom_survey.cyclonedx.validate() survives only as a cross-check required to AGREE IN BOTH DIRECTIONS.",
+    "FIX-2 [P2] 2/3 (round 2, STILL CLOSED) — fixture exclusion was not exercised AT THE SURVEY BOUNDARY. AC-SBOM-06 drives BOTH tier maps through run_survey(..., tier_map=...). This is the ORIGINAL instance of the class fix-4 swept for.",
+    "FIX-2 [P2] 3/3 (round 2, STILL CLOSED) — determinism was tested only on the PINNED-timestamp path. AC-SBOM-22 runs THREE legs and requires the default metadata.timestamp to be MORE THAN 24 HOURS from the real clock.",
+    "FIX-2 [P3] 4/4 (round 2, STILL CLOSED) — stale manifest count 28 -> 29 everywhere, and the number is DERIVED: §8 gate 9 re-runs the grep (rc=0, prints 29, re-confirmed at fix-5).",
+    "FIX-1 [P2] 1/3 (round 1, STILL CLOSED) — `setup.cfg` missing from the manifest oracle; MANIFEST_BASENAMES re-diffed against the design table and split into 'matches today' / 'forward-looking' with a lockstep warning on both sides. That helper is now ALSO the derivation source for AC-SBOM-21's untiered set (fix-5), which is why fix-5 did not write a second list.",
+    "FIX-1 [P2] 2/3 (round 1, STILL CLOSED) — no `purl` assertion. AC-SBOM-10 asserts purl present, ecosystem prefix, bom-ref == purl, an @version segment equal to the component's own locked version, and a document-level purl-type set of EXACTLY {cargo, npm, pypi}.",
+    "FIX-1 [P2] 3/3 (round 1, STILL CLOSED) — dependency-graph coverage was vacuous. AC-SBOM-12 asserts the array is non-empty, closed in BOTH directions, carries at least one library<->library edge, and lands at least one of five pinned lockfile-only crates tagged `transitive`.",
+    "FIX-1 STEER 1 (round 1, still in force) — .github/dependabot.yml is the tracked source of record for the tiering; dev/tools/mermaid/package.json is surfaced as a tracked npm manifest under NO configured Dependabot directory and handed to Slice 33 §4 / 0.8.22, since 0.8.20 may not touch .github/**.",
+    "FIX-1 STEER 2 (round 1, still in force) — findings home RULED to dev/plans/runs/0.8.20-slice-33-library-sweep-3-FINDINGS.md; AC-SBOM-18's expected SLICE_33_FINDINGS_DOC moved with the ruling and the path was verified NOT gitignored.",
+    "Round-0 content unchanged and still in force: plan-0.8.20.md §3a leg 1/3 — requirements + acceptance criteria + design + RED tests, NO implementation code, delivered as dev/design/0.8.20-slice-31-sbom-survey-tool.md (§3 REQ-1..REQ-14, §4 the 23 AC->test map, §5 the design) plus scripts/sbom-survey/{README.md,tests/}. §5.1 git-ls-files discovery with python/pyproject.toml UNTRACKED and that scope asserted at the survey boundary; §5.2 data-driven fixture exclusion proven at the survey boundary; §5.3 longest-prefix-wins tiering with NO catch-all, a loud UntieredManifestError, an oracle for the matching promises themselves, the BOM's tier required to equal the rules' answer, and — from fix-5 — the WHICH-PATH-WHEN-SEVERAL rule that separates the unit criterion from the CLI one; §5.4 injectable published-version seam that run_survey must actually ask; §5.5 lockfile-parse edges with no cargo-metadata/npm-ls subprocess; §5.7 named external libraries; §5.8 the Slice-33 consumer field set and determinism, graded on the written artifact. No part of Slice 33 was built."
